        <body>
            <div xml:id="d568">

                <pb facs="0603" n="487" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg487"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 838.00/1295.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Haitian
                    Minister</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-09-10">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 10, 1915</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have your two letters dated the 4th and
                    6th instant, respectively.</p>
                <p>It will, I am sure, be perfectly apparent to you that your two letters and our
                    informal conversations can, under present circumstances, only be considered as
                    having an unofficial character.</p>
                <p>With every desire to preclude the possibility of any future embarrassment, I must
                    again repeat my earnest conviction that the interests of Haiti will best be
                    served by restricting pending negotiations to Port au Prince, exclusively.</p>
                <p>I remain [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
